Synchronet OpenID Connect provider
==================================

These Synchronet web server scripts let a client sign in a BBS user and
receive a signed ID token identifying that user by alias. The scripts
implement the provider side of the exchange. Two details matter before
configuring a client: discovery advertises HTTP URLs while ID tokens name
an HTTPS issuer, and the access tokens have no backing resource service.

Install the endpoints
---------------------

Use the document root served for the BBS host. Paths on the left below
are relative to the source tree's web/root/oauth directory.

    Source                           Destination under the site's web root
    auth.ssjs, token.ssjs, jwks.ssjs  oauth/ (keep each filename)
    rewrite_lib.js, webctrl.ini       oauth/ (authorization and token routes)
    install/openid-configuration.ssjs .well-known/openid-configuration.ssjs
    install/rewrite_lib.js            .well-known/rewrite_lib.js (merge)
    install/webctrl.ini               .well-known/webctrl.ini (see below)

If the site already serves oauth/ from the source tree, those five files
are already in place. Create .well-known/ if needed. The two files named
rewrite_lib.js contain different functions, each loaded by the webctrl.ini
in its own directory.

For discovery, copy openid-configuration.ssjs, add oid_conf_rewrite() to
the destination rewrite_lib.js, and merge the [openid-configuration]
section from install/webctrl.ini into the destination webctrl.ini. On a
new site the discovery rewrite library can be copied as a file.

The top-level AccessRequirements=level 90 in install/webctrl.ini applies
to the staging directory. Copying it into .well-known/ would restrict
discovery and could also restrict other services there. For public
discovery, the metadata must be readable without a BBS login. Preserve
the site's other access rules and check the inherited rules as well.

The installation under /synchronet/sbbs/web/root has both discovery and
WebFinger in .well-known/. Its webctrl.ini has separate [webfinger] and
[openid-configuration] sections, and its rewrite_lib.js contains both
functions. WebFinger is an additional installed service. The files in
this install directory supply only OpenID provider metadata.

Host name and signing key
-------------------------

The scripts use system.host_name for the issuer, endpoint URLs and RSA key
label. They require Synchronet SSJS with CryptKeyset and CryptContext.
All three oauth/*.ssjs handlers read the system password from the legacy
binary main.cnf in system.ctrl_dir, even when a signing key already
exists. A system with only main.ini requires a script update before use.

The first OAuth handler invoked creates oid.key in system.ctrl_dir if
needed and generates a 2048-bit RSA key if it cannot load the labeled
key. The web server account needs access to main.cnf and the keyset, and
permission to create the keyset on first use. Keep oid.key private and
preserve it across upgrades. Clients fetch its public key from jwks.ssjs.

Check discovery and the public key
----------------------------------

Replace bbs.example with the BBS host. From a client without a BBS login,
request both documents over HTTPS.

    curl -fsS https://bbs.example/.well-known/openid-configuration
    curl -fsS https://bbs.example/oauth/jwks.ssjs

Discovery should return JSON with issuer, authorization_endpoint,
token_endpoint and jwks_uri. If only the extensionless URL gives a 404,
try /.well-known/openid-configuration.ssjs and check the discovery rewrite.
A login challenge on discovery points to the effective access requirements.
The key response should contain a keys array with an RSA public key,
algorithm RS256 and key ID "1". If discovery works but the key request
fails, check the web server log for a main.cnf or keyset error.

As shipped, discovery builds its issuer and all three URLs with http://,
even when fetched over HTTPS. ID tokens use https:// for the same host.
Clients that compare issuers will reject that mismatch. Before using
discovery for sign-in, align the issuer in openid-configuration.ssjs with
the public HTTPS issuer. The three advertised endpoints derive from it.

Sign in with an authorization code
----------------------------------

1. Send the user's browser to /oauth/auth.ssjs on the HTTPS BBS host with
   URL-encoded response_type=code, client_id and redirect_uri parameters.
   Include scope=openid, a fresh state for the callback and a nonce for
   the ID token. The [auth.ssjs] rule requires a BBS login (level 0).
   /oauth/authorize is an alias when followed by a query string.

2. After login, the browser receives a 302 to redirect_uri with code and
   the supplied state in the query string. The client checks the state
   and has ten minutes from code issuance to exchange the code.

3. POST form-encoded grant_type=authorization_code, code, client_id and
   redirect_uri to /oauth/token.ssjs over HTTPS. Use the same client ID
   and callback URI as in step 1. The /oauth/token alias also works when
   the URL ends in /token and the parameters are in the request body.
   The JSON response contains id_token, access_token, token_type (Bearer)
   and expires_in (3600 seconds).

4. Verify the ID token's RS256 signature with the published key, then
   check issuer, audience, expiry and the nonce sent in step 1. The token
   carries the BBS alias in sub, the client ID in aud, and the issue and
   expiry times in iat and exp. auth_time is set to the time of issuance.

Other responses and limits
--------------------------

auth.ssjs also accepts id_token, token and the space-separated value
"id_token token". These responses return their fields in the redirect
URI fragment. Discovery lists "token id_token" in the opposite order
and omits the token-only response. Use "code" for the flow above.

The scripts do not register clients, validate callback URIs against a
registry, or bind a code to its issuing client and callback. The token
handler checks the code's age but does not record redemption. There is
no client-secret or PKCE check. These limits matter when deciding which
applications can be trusted with BBS sign-in. The access token is a
generated string that the scripts neither store nor validate. The
package supplies no UserInfo endpoint or refresh-token grant.
